ubjects. Use me, him, her, us or them as objects. You and it keep the same form. Focus on **possessive determiner** in this question.","explanation":"Use your before the noun useful tool to identify the owner.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"possessive-determiner","contextKey":"owner-useful-tool","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-pronouns","bc-possessives","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"01ce7d1a8b9b23e9010f77380fa2cd531746b32dac7fe4ac9a4656b1769ce001","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **possessive determiner**. Use I, he, she, we or they as subjects. Use me, him, her, us or them as objects. You and it keep the same form.","Use your before the noun useful tool to identify the owner.","Choose **your**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**her** can be a possessive determiner, but it refers to a different person or number from the one specified. Use your before the noun useful tool to identify the owner.","B":"Use your before the noun useful tool to identify the owner.","C":"**my** can be a possessive determiner, but it refers to a different person or number from the one specified. Use your before the noun useful tool to identify the owner.","D":"**his** can be a possessive determiner, but it refers to a different person or number from the one specified. Use your before the noun useful tool to identify the owner."}}},{"id":"27bdd03d-70b7-581d-a1aa-c14deda77730","topicSlug":"pronouns-and-possessive-determiners","level":"A1","prompt":"The useful tool belongs to you. Complete without repeating the noun: The useful tool is ____.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"mine"},{"id":"B","text":"his"},{"id":"C","text":"hers"},{"id":"D","text":"yours"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Use I, he, she, we or they as subjects. Use me, him, her, us or them as objects. You and it keep the same form. Focus on **possessive pronoun** in this question.","explanation":"Use yours on its own to replace the possessive noun phrase.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"possessive-pronoun","contextKey":"owner-useful-tool","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-pronouns","bc-possessives","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"d5028f2e7060fadf9d447ac7076cb8c75bd124c9cab7540031746cc9d628cc74","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **possessive pronoun**. Use I, he, she, we or they as subjects. Use me, him, her, us or them as objects. You and it keep the same form.","Use yours on its own to replace the possessive noun phrase.","Choose **yours**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**mine** can be a possessive pronoun, but it refers to a different person or number from the one specified. Use yours on its own to replace the possessive noun phrase.","B":"**his** can be a possessive pronoun, but it refers to a different person or number from the one specified. Use yours on its own to replace the possessive noun phrase.","C":"**hers** can be a possessive pronoun, but it refers to a different person or number from the one specified. Use yours on its own to replace the possessive noun phrase.","D":"Use yours on its own to replace the possessive noun phrase."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Pronouns avoid repeating a noun while keeping clear who or what is being discussed. Their form depends on the job they do in the sentence. English distinguishes subject forms, object forms, possessive determiners before nouns and possessive pronouns that stand alone."],"rules":[{"title":"Subject and object","explanation":"Use I, he, she, we or they as subjects. Use me, him, her, us or them as objects. You and it keep the same form.","examples":["She invited us.","They spoke to me.","**They** invited **us** to **their** house."],"pattern":"**I / he / she / we / they** → subject","paragraphs":["A subject pronoun is the subject of a verb: She called. An object pronoun can be the object of a verb or a preposition: I called her; I spoke to her. In formal standard usage, adding another person does not change the case: Sam and I called, but they called Sam and me."]},{"title":"Before a noun","explanation":"Use my, your, his, her, its, our or their directly before a noun. These determiners identify the owner.","examples":["Our appointment is tomorrow.","Her parcel has arrived.","This notebook is **mine**; the other one is **yours**."],"pattern":"**me / him / her / us / them** → object","paragraphs":["A possessive determiner comes before a noun: my coat, our seats, their plan. A possessive pronoun stands in place of the whole noun phrase: the coat is mine. His has the same form in both jobs, so its position and the following noun reveal its function."]},{"title":"Replacing a noun phrase","explanation":"Mine, yours, his, hers, ours and theirs stand on their own. Do not add an apostrophe. His works both before a noun and on its own.","examples":["This seat is yours.","Those gloves are theirs.","The dog wagged **its** tail because **it’s** happy."],"pattern":"**my book** → **mine** · **their book** → **theirs**","paragraphs":["Possessive pronouns do not take apostrophes: yours, hers, ours, theirs. It’s means it is or it has; its is possessive. You can refer to one or more people, and singular they is commonly used for someone whose gender is unknown or who uses they."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"Keep the referent clear when several people are mentioned. If two possible antecedents make she ambiguous, repeat a name. Reflexive pronouns such as herself have a different role: they normally connect an object back to the same subject, or add emphasis.","examples":["**They** invited **us** to **their** house.","This notebook is **mine**; the other one is **yours**.","The dog wagged **its** tail because **it’s** happy."]}],"recap":["**I / he / she / we / they** → subject","**me / him / her / us / them** → object","**my book** → **mine** · **their book** → **theirs**"]},"stage":"1 · Sentence foundations","prerequisites":["nouns-and-plurals"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
